              "id": "c2-s1-i-cand2",
              "authorStyle": "Rothfuss / Inoue",
              "content": "The wooden door swung open, and my mother hurried inside carrying a rough clay bowl covered with a clean rag. Dropping to her knees, she grabbed me under the armpits and hauled me back onto the straw mat as if I were a sack of dry grain.\n\n\"I was... meditating, Mother,\" I wheezed, trying to maintain some shred of dignity.\n\n\"You were eating dirt,\" Yeon said bluntly, wiping a smear of mud from my chin with her sleeve. \"Lie still. The apothecary said the bone behind your ear is still tender. If you start twitching like that, the humors will unbalance.\"\n\nShe set the bowl down between us. Inside was a pale, watery broth with three dried minnows floating among pieces of boiled marsh weed. It didn't smell like much, but my stomach let out a ferocious growl that echoed through the entire room.\n\nYeon smiled—a faint, tired thing that barely touched her dark eyes—and pressed a wooden spoon into my hand.\n\n\"Eat. Your father traded our evening salt ration to get those minnows from the south wharf.\"\n\nMy hand stopped halfway to the bowl.\n\n\"He traded the salt?\"\n\nIn a frontier settlement like Southfall, salt wasn't seasoning. It was currency. Without salt, you couldn't preserve fish for the winter frosts, and you couldn't trade for iron nails at the central forge.\n\n\"Your father cares more about your ribs knitting than salt in a barrel,\" Yeon said softly. She looked down at her lap, her calloused fingers unconsciously pleating the hem of her apron. \"Drink it all, Jin. He took the double watch at the trench today so Sector Chief Kang wouldn't mark down our household.\"",
