              "authorStyle": "Rothfuss / Inoue",
              "content": "I swallowed the broth in four gulps. It tasted of bitter mud and river weeds, but as the warmth trickled down into my belly, I didn't let it waste.\n\nClosing my eyes, I drew a slow, deliberate breath through my teeth. \n\nI didn't try to force Qi through my blocked meridians. Forcing a clogged pipe only bursts the seams. Instead, I used the oldest technique of the Mount Hua sect—the **Gentle Breath of the Morning Dew**. I guided the meager nutrients from the fish into the marrow of my cracked ribs, knitting the hairline fractures with the stubborn friction of living blood.\n\nIt was slow. It was agonizing. \n\nEvery breath sent a dull ache radiating through my chest, but by the tenth cycle, the trembling in my fingers had ceased.\n\nAn hour later, Yeon gathered her willow basket and went down to the river to scrub the communal linen.\n\nThe moment the wooden latch clicked behind her, I was on my feet.\n\nMy knees wobbled, but they held. I picked up a simple bamboo walking pole from the corner of the room, tucked a small stone-chisel from my father's workbench into my waistband, and pushed the door open.",
