              "content": "Southfall Village was not a village.\n\nIt was a fortress built by desperate men with dull tools.\n\nThe huts—clumps of unhewn logs, turf roofs, and mud-chinked walls—were clustered tightly inside a massive circular palisade made of sharpened pine trunks. Above the pointed stakes, the sky hung gray and heavy, smelling of crushed pine needles, woodsmoke, and the sour stench of beast dung.\n\nEvery twenty paces along the inner wall, men with drawn, hollow faces stood guard holding crude spears tipped with chipped flint or rusted iron. Their eyes weren't watching the village streets. They were staring out through the murder-holes into the dense, black treeline beyond the trench.\n\nI leaned on my bamboo pole, shuffling through the mud of the central lane like a half-healed invalid. \n\nNobody paid attention to a scrawny boy with a bandaged head. They were too busy working. Two men dragged a dead, six-legged wolf across the square, its coarse gray fur matted with black, tar-like blood. A blacksmith's hammer rang out rhythmically from an open-sided shed, reshaping a bent iron pitchfork.\n\n*The beasts are aggressive,* I observed, reading the signs like a seasoned scout. *The claw marks on the palisade aren't random scrambles. They're targeted. Every three feet along the north gate, the wood has been scraped at the exact same height. As if something was measuring the grain.*\n\n\"Stand fast, men of the Third Sector!\"\n\nA booming, resonant voice cut across the muster yard.\n\nI paused behind the timber frame of a grain silo, peering around the corner.\n\nIn the center of the square, standing on an elevated wooden platform above the defensive trenches, was a man in polished iron mail. He was tall, thick in the chest, with a well-groomed black beard and a red sash tied across his shoulder.",
